n person saying come with  us come with us in English which they  don't speak so they're very uh skeptical  and quite afraid  um we've had people basically say sorry  we don't want to come with you because  they're terrified we're going to take  you into the woods and shoot them  because they think we could be Russian  because they have no idea what has been  happening in the outside world because  they've just been locked in this little  bubble of this village with no outside  cons no ukrainians there to talk to them  or soldiers or anything so they've just  been sat in their basement for what  seven or eight months with no outside  information so suddenly when a van  appears saying get in the van we're  taking you to safety like would they  believe us normally probably not  yeah so that that is tough and tell us a  little bit more about where you take  them do you take them out of the country  or do you take them to hospitals still  within Ukraine  so uh our group kind of um specializes  in little runs to the the front so we  will basically drive like an hour from  here in Providence to the front line  Villages take them uh basically out to a  nearby town which is uh they've set up  um uh a hospice which is now converted  into sort of refugee Center for these  people who have had to leave all of  their belongings and we get to take we  take them there and then they have um  kind of nurses there who look after them  for a few weeks whilst they kind of like  sort their lives out  and as I understand it you your job as a  running coach in Cheltenham so how are  you finding it there in Ukraine you talk  about going to the front line the new  front line that must be extremely  dangerous how how are you handling it  um  I don't know I think uh maybe I'm a bit  of an odd one but I kind of take each  day as it comes  um sometimes when you see some pretty  terrible things it does stay with you uh  for example seeing kind of uh  the burnt out corpse of uh her mother  who we were evacuating  um it was supposed to be evacuating her  and her daughter but unfortunately the  day before we arrived their houses was  hit so she the daughter very sadly burnt  to death and the mother was she was just  uh she was like a ghost  um so that that does stick with you but  in the moment you know you've got a job  um you've got a you're in a position of  care as soon as you pick these people up  you've got to just get out um and get  away from sort of the artillery or the  drones which is kind of constantly going  off around us so  um because of the intensity in the  moment it's fine but it's kind of when  you get back and you start to like  think oh that was kind of close that was  only 100 meters away from us and that's  when you kind of think okay maybe uh  maybe my luck might run out but it's  worth it to save these people I think  and in the meantime you're trying to  raise money aren't you for for a new  vehicle tell us a bit about that and why  you need it yeah so um in the summer it  was kind of fine going around in the van  because the roads I mean the roads don't  they're not roads they're just like  tractor Trails but it's okay because  it's hard whereas now I mean you might  be able to hear it's trench or rain and  all the tanks going up and down on these  um on these tractor Trails it's just a  quagmire so  um I've been getting stuck quite  recently and with the winter coming with  snow and the ice this van just won't be  able to get to the places anymore  um and as the Russians are pushed back  in this area uh the roads are only going  to deteriorates so we're trying to  fundraise uh fourway four by four so  that we can actually keep the work  through the winter because for these  people the winter is the hardest time  because it's hitting like minus 50 minus  20. um even at the moment it's starting  to go below zero and just us trying to  find these houses we're getting pretty  chilly let alone being sat in the  basement for months and months  okay and you've got to go find me a page  for that haven't you um well Chris Parry  thanks so much for talking to us this  morning good luck with your work and  stay safe thank you thank you very much
 

